<p>The first call has one job: to understand what they need well enough to agree a sensible next step. It is not a pitch.</p>

<h2>A shape that works</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Open</strong> (15 seconds): who you are, why you’re calling, and check it’s a good time. <em>“Hi Sam, it’s Alex from Brightside. You got in touch about your garden. Is now still OK?”</em></li>
  <li><strong>Set the agenda</strong>: <em>“I’ll ask a few questions so I understand what you need, then tell you how we could help and what happens next.”</em> People relax when they know how the call will go.</li>
  <li><strong>Discover</strong>: ask, then listen. Aim for them talking twice as much as you. See <em>Questions that uncover what they need</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Play it back</strong>: <em>“So what you need is …, and what matters most is …. Did I get that right?”</em> This is the moment people decide you understand them.</li>
  <li><strong>Agree the next step</strong>: a visit, a quote, a second call, with a date. Don’t end with “I’ll be in touch”.</li>
</ol>

<h2>After the call</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Add a note on the lead with what you learned, in their words.</li>
  <li>Mark the call done with <em>Reached them</em> (the lead becomes <em>Contacted</em>), and move it to <em>Qualified</em> once you know they need what you do, can decide, and have the time and budget.</li>
  <li>Send the <em>Recap after a call</em> email the same day, and set the next step as you mark the call done.</li>
</ul>

<h2>If it isn’t a fit</h2>
<p>Say so kindly, and if you can, point them somewhere better. Mark the lead <em>Lost</em> with a note about why. A good “no” is remembered and recommended.</p>
